== Chapter VI

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The Mortification of Sin in particular described. The several Parts and Degrees thereof.] 1. #emph[The habitual weakening of its Root and Principle. The Power of Lust to tempt. Differences of that Power to Persons and Times.] 2. #emph[Constant Fighting against Sin. The Parts thereof considered.] 3. #emph[Success against it. The sum of this Discourse.]]
]

==== 2. Positively: What It Is

#emph[#smallcaps[What] it is to mortify a Sin] in General, which will make farther way for #emph[particular Directions,] is nextly to be considered.

The #emph[Mortification] of a #emph[Lust] consists in three things.

1\. An #emph[habitual] weakening of it. Every Lust is a depraved #emph[habit] or Disposition, #emph[continually] inclining the Heart to evil: Thence is that description of him who hath no Lust truly mortified: Gen 6:5. #emph[Every Imagination of the thoughts of his Heart is only evil continually.] He is always under the power of a strong bent and Inclination to sin. And the reason why a #emph[natural man] is not always, perpetually, in the pursuit of some #emph[one lust] night and day, is, because he hath #emph[many] to serve, every one crying to be satisfied; Thence he is carried on with great #emph[variety,] but still in general he lies towards the satisfaction of #emph[self.]

We will suppose then the #emph[lust] or distemper whose Mortification is inquired after, to be in itself a #emph[strong, deeply] rooted, #emph[habitual] Inclination and bent of Will and Affections unto some Actual sin, as to the Matter of it, (though not under that #emph[formal] Consideration,) always stirring up #emph[Imaginations, Thoughts] and #emph[Contrivances] about the Object of it. Hence men are said to have their Hearts #emph[set] upon Evil, Rom 13:14 the bent of their Spirits lies towards it, to make #emph[provision for the flesh.] And a #emph[sinful depraved habit] (as in many other things, so in this) differs from all #emph[natural] or #emph[moral habits] whatever; for whereas they incline the Soul gently and suitably to itself, #emph[sinful] Habits impel with Violence and Impetuousness:#footnote[Στρατεύονται κατὰ τῆς ψυχῆς· ἀντιστρατευόμενον· αἰχμαλωτίζοντα.] whence Lusts are said to #emph[fight] or #emph[wage war] against the Soul, 1 Pet 2:11 to #emph[rebel,] or rise up in War with that Conduct and Opposition which is usual therein; Rom 7:23 to lead captive, or effectually captivating upon success in battle: All works of great violence and impetuousness.

I might manifest fully from that #emph[description] we have of it, Rom 7. How it will darken the Mind, extinguish #emph[Convictions, dethrone] Reason, #emph[interrupt] the Power and Influence of any Considerations that may be brought to hamper it, and break through all into a flame. But this is not my present business. Now the first thing in #emph[Mortification] is the #emph[weakening] of this #emph[Habit] of Sin or Lust, that it shall not with that #emph[violence, earnestness, frequency,] rise up, #emph[conceive,] tumultuate, provoke, #emph[entice,] disquiet, as naturally it is apt to do, Jas 1:14, 15.

I shall desire to give one #emph[Caution] or Rule by the way; and it is this. Though every Lust doth in its own Nature, #emph[equally, universally] incline and impel to Sin, yet this must be granted with these two Limitations:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. One #emph[lust,] or a #emph[lust] in one man, may receive many accidental Improvements, #emph[heightenings,] and #emph[strengthenings,] which may give it #emph[life, power] and #emph[vigor,] exceedingly above what another Lust hath, or the #emph[same lust,] that is of the same Kind and Nature in #emph[another] man. When a Lust falls in with the #emph[natural Constitution] and Temper, with a #emph[suitable] course of Life, with #emph[Occasions]\; or when Satan hath got a fit #emph[handle] to it to manage it, as he hath a thousand #emph[ways] so to do, that Lust grows violent and impetuous above others, or more than the #emph[same lust] in another man; Then the #emph[steams] of it darken the Mind, so that though a man #emph[knows] the same things as formerly, yet they have no power, nor influence on the Will, but corrupt Affections and Passions are set by it at liberty.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But especially, Lust gets strength by #emph[Temptation]\; when a suitable Temptation falls in with a Lust, it gives it a new life, vigor, power, #emph[violence] and rage which it seemed not before to have, or to be capable of. Instances to this purpose might be multiplied; but it is the design of some part of another Treatise to evince this Observation.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Some #emph[lusts] are far more #emph[sensible] and #emph[discernible] in their violent Actings than others. #emph[Paul] puts a difference between Uncleanness and all other sins. 1 Cor 6:18. #emph[Flee fornication, every sin that a man doth, is without the body, but he that committeth fornication sinneth against his own body.] Hence the Motions of that sin are more #emph[sensible,] more #emph[discernible] than of others; when perhaps, the #emph[love of the World,] or the like, is in a Person no less #emph[habitually] predominant than that, yet it makes not so great a #emph[combustion] in the whole man.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And on this account some men may go in their own thoughts and in the eyes of the World, for #emph[mortified] men; who yet have in them no less predominancy of Lust, than those who cry out with Astonishment upon the account of its perplexing Tumultuatings. Yea than those who have by the Power of it, been hurried into scandalous sins; only their #emph[lusts] are in and about things which raise not such a tumult in the Soul, about which they are exercised with a calmer Frame of Spirit; the very Fabric of Nature being not so nearly concerned in them, as in some other.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I say then, that the first thing in Mortification is the #emph[weakening] of this Habit, that it shall not impel and tumultuate as formerly, that it shall not entice and draw aside, that it shall not #emph[disquiet] and #emph[perplex]\; the killing of its #emph[life,] vigor, promptness and readiness to be stirring. This is called #emph[crucifying the flesh with the lusts thereof,] Gal 5:24 that is, taking away its #emph[Blood and Spirits] that give it strength and power. The #emph[wasting] of the #emph[body of Death day by day,] 2 Cor 4:16.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As a Man #emph[nailed] to the #emph[Cross]\; he first struggles and strives and cries out with great strength and might; but as his #emph[Blood] and #emph[Spirits] waste, his #emph[strivings] are #emph[faint] and seldom, his #emph[Cries low] and hoarse, scarce to be heard. When a man first sets on a Lust or distemper, to deal with it, it #emph[struggles] with great #emph[violence] to break loose; it cries with Earnestness and Impatiency to be #emph[satisfied] and relieved; but when by #emph[Mortification] the #emph[blood] and #emph[spirits] of it are #emph[let] out, it moves #emph[seldom] and #emph[familiarly,] cries #emph[sparingly,] and is scarce heard in the #emph[heart]\; It may have sometimes a #emph[dying pang,] that makes an Appearance of great #emph[vigor] and strength, but it is #emph[quickly over,] especially if it be kept from #emph[considerable] success. This the Apostle describes as in the whole Chapter, so especially v. 6 of ch. 6. #emph[Rom.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Sin,] saith he, is #emph[crucified]\; it is fastened to the Cross; to what End? That the #emph[body of Death may be destroyed]\; the Power of Sin weakened, and abolished by little and little; that #emph[henceforth we should not serve sin,] that is, that sin might not incline, impel us with such Efficacy, as to #emph[make] us #emph[servants] to it, as it hath done heretofore. And this is spoken not only with respect to #emph[carnal] and sensual Affections, or Desires of worldly things; not only in respect of the Lust of the Flesh, the Lust of the Eyes, and the #emph[pride] of Life, but also as to the #emph[flesh,] that is in the #emph[mind] and #emph[will,] in that Opposition unto God, which is in us by Nature. Of what Nature soever the troubling Distemper be, by what ways soever it make itself out, either by #emph[impelling] to Evil, or #emph[hindering] from that which is good, the Rule is the same. And unless this be done effectually, all after-contention will not compass the End aimed at. A man may beat down the #emph[bitter] Fruit from an #emph[evil] Tree, until he is weary; whilst the Root abides in strength and vigor, the beating down of the present #emph[Fruit] will not hinder it from bringing forth more; this is the Folly of some men; they set themselves with all earnestness and diligence against the appearing eruption of #emph[lust,] but leaving the Principle and Root #emph[untouched,] perhaps unsearched out, they make but little or no progress in this work of Mortification.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. In constant #emph[fighting] and #emph[contending] against sin. To be able always to be #emph[laying load] on Sin, is no small #emph[degree] of #emph[Mortification.] When Sin is #emph[strong] and vigorous, the Soul is scarce able to #emph[make] any #emph[head] against it. It #emph[sighs,] and #emph[groans,] and #emph[mourns,] and is troubled, as #emph[David] speaks of himself, but seldom has Sin in the #emph[pursuit; David] complains that his Sin had taken #emph[fast hold upon him, that he could not look up,] Ps 40:12. How #emph[little] then was he able to fight against it. Now sundry things are #emph[required] unto, and #emph[comprised] in this fighting against Sin.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. To #emph[know] that a man hath such an Enemy to deal withal; to take notice of it, to consider it as an #emph[Enemy] indeed, and one that is to be destroyed by all means possible, is required hereunto. As I said before, the contest is vigorous and hazardous; it is about the things of #emph[Eternity.] When therefore men have #emph[slight] and transient thoughts of their #emph[lusts,] it is no great #emph[sign] that they are #emph[mortified,] or that they are in a way for their Mortification. This is, every man's knowing #emph[the plague of his own heart,] 1 Kgs 8:38. Without which no other work can be done; it is to be feared that very many have little knowledge of the main #emph[Enemy,] that they carry about them in their bosoms. This makes them ready to justify themselves, and to be impatient of reproof or admonition, not knowing that they are in any danger, 2 Chr 16:10.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. To labor to be acquainted with the Ways, Wiles, Methods, Advantages and Occasions of its #emph[success] is the beginning of this #emph[warfare.] So do men deal with Enemies. They inquire out their #emph[counsels] and #emph[designs,] ponder their Ends, consider how and by what #emph[means] they have formerly #emph[prevailed,] that they may be prevented; In this consists the greatest #emph[skill] in conduct. Take this away, and all #emph[waging] of War (wherein is the greatest improvement of Human #emph[Wisdom] and Industry) would be #emph[brutish.] So do they deal with Lust, who mortify it indeed; not only when it is actually #emph[vexing, enticing,] and #emph[seducing,] but in their Retirements they consider, This is our Enemy, this is his way and Progress, these are his Advantages, thus hath he prevailed, and thus he will do, if not prevented: So #emph[David, My sin is ever before me,] Ps 51:3. And indeed one of the choicest and most eminent parts of #emph[practically] spiritual wisdom, consists in finding out the subtleties, policies, and depths of any indwelling Sin; to consider and know wherein its greatest #emph[strength] lies; what Advantage it uses to make of Occasions, Opportunities, Temptations; what are its #emph[Pleas,] Pretenses, Reasonings; what its Stratagems, Colors, Excuses; to set the #emph[wisdom] of the Spirit against the Craft of the #emph[old Man,] to trace this Serpent in all its turnings and windings; to be able to say at its most secret, and (to a common Frame of Heart) imperceptible actings, This is your old way and course, I know what you aim at; and so to be always in readiness, is a good part of our warfare.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. To load it daily with all the things which shall after be mentioned, that are #emph[grievous, killing] and #emph[destructive] to it, is the height of this #emph[contest]\; such an one never thinks his #emph[lust dead] because it is Quiet, but labors still to give it new #emph[wounds,] new #emph[blows] every day. So the Apostle, Col 3:5.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now whilst the Soul is in this Condition, whilst it is thus dealing, it is certainly #emph[uppermost,] Sin is under the Sword and #emph[dying.]]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. In #emph[success]\; frequent #emph[success] against any #emph[lust,] is another part and Evidence of #emph[Mortification]\; By #emph[success,] I understand not a mere disappointment of Sin, that it be not #emph[brought forth,] nor accomplished; but a #emph[victory] over it, and pursuit of it to a #emph[complete] Conquest: For instance, when the Heart finds #emph[Sin] at any time at work, #emph[seducing,] forming #emph[Imaginations] to make #emph[provision] for the flesh, to fulfill the lusts thereof, it instantly apprehends Sin, and brings it to the #emph[Law] of God, and #emph[love] of Christ; #emph[condemns] it, #emph[follows] it with #emph[execution] to the uttermost.]

Now I say, when a man comes to this #emph[state] and Condition, that #emph[lust] is #emph[weakened] in the #emph[root] and #emph[principle,] that its #emph[motions] and #emph[actions] are #emph[fewer] and #emph[weaker] than formerly, so that they are not able to hinder his #emph[duty,] nor interrupt his peace, when he can in a quiet #emph[sedate] frame of Spirit, #emph[find] out, and fight against Sin, and have #emph[success] against it, then Sin is #emph[mortified] in some considerable #emph[measure,] and notwithstanding all its Opposition, a man may have peace with God all his days.

Unto these Heads then do I refer the #emph[Mortification] aimed at; that is, of any one #emph[perplexing Distemper,] whereby the General #emph[pravity] and Corruption of our Nature #emph[attempts] to exert and put forth itself.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. First, the #emph[weakening] of its indwelling #emph[disposition,] whereby it #emph[inclines, entices, impels] to evil, #emph[rebels, opposes, fights] against God, by the #emph[implanting] habitual #emph[residence,] and cherishing of a #emph[principle] of Grace, that stands in direct #emph[opposition] to it, and is destructive of it, is the Foundation of it. So by the #emph[implanting and growth] of #emph[humility] is #emph[pride] weakened, #emph[passion] by #emph[patience, uncleanness] by #emph[purity] of Mind and Conscience, #emph[love] of this world by #emph[heavenly-mindedness,] which are #emph[Graces] of the Spirit, or the same #emph[habitual] Grace variously acting itself by the Holy Ghost, according to the #emph[variety] or diversity of the #emph[Objects] about which it is exercised; as the other are #emph[several] Lusts, or the same #emph[natural Corruption] variously acting itself according to the various Advantages and Occasions that it meets withal.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The #emph[promptness, alacrity,] vigor of the Spirit, or #emph[New Man] in contending with, cheerful fighting against the Lust spoken of, by all the Ways, and with all the Means that are appointed thereunto, constantly using the #emph[succors] provided against its #emph[motions] and #emph[actings,] is a second thing hereunto required.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[Success] unto #emph[several degrees] attends these two. Now this, if the distemper hath not an unconquerable Advantage from its natural situation, may possibly be to such an #emph[universal] Conquest, as the Soul may never more sensibly feel its Opposition, and shall however assuredly arise to an allowance of #emph[Peace] to the #emph[Conscience,] according to the tenor of the #emph[Covenant] of Grace.]]
